{n:1, t:"Training Text", p:"Give the model one sentence to learn from.", w:"A language model learns from examples of text, so we need text. Our entire dataset is a single famous sentence that contains every letter of the alphabet — small enough to inspect completely, rich enough to learn from.", inp:"One raw sentence: <span class='mono'>The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog</span> (9 words).", proc:"We keep the sentence exactly as written. Capital The and lowercase the are treated as different words — the model only sees characters.", out:"One clean training sentence. Everything in this course traces back to these 9 words.", m:["Dataset","1 sentence = the entire training set"], eli5:"It is like giving a student one page to study for the whole test.", pic:"Imagine a single strip of paper with nine words on it, sliding into a small machine. Everything the machine ever learns comes from that strip.", rem:["The entire dataset is ONE sentence.","Every number in this course comes from these 9 words."], qz:[{q:"What is the training data for this model?",a:"One sentence: 'The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.' That is the whole dataset."},{q:"How many words does the model see?",a:"9 words. Capital 'The' and lowercase 'the' count as different words."}], rv:{inp:"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog",out:"9 word tokens",q:"What happened to the sentence?",a:"It was split into individual words (tokens). That is tokenization — the very next step."}, mem:{one:"One sentence is the whole dataset.",img:"A single page of text feeding a small machine.",eq:"dataset = 1 sentence",oneR:"Every weight and gradient in this course comes from 9 words.",recall:"How many words are in the training sentence?"}},

