{n:5, t:"Reverse Vocabulary", p:"Build the ID→word lookup so numbers can become words again.", w:"The model outputs numbers (probabilities over IDs), but humans read words. The reverse vocabulary translates the winning ID back into text — essential for prediction and text generation later.", inp:"The vocabulary (word → ID).", proc:"Flip each pair: 0→The, 1→quick, 2→brown, 3→fox, 4→jumps, 5→over, 6→the, 7→lazy, 8→dog.", out:"A lookup table from ID back to word.", m:["Lookup","ID → word"], eli5:"A decoder ring: the model whispers a number, and you translate it back into a word.", pic:"A two-way street sign: words on one side, numbers on the other.", rem:["Vocabulary: word → ID. Reverse vocabulary: ID → word.","Generation needs this to print words instead of numbers."], qz:[{q:"Why do we need the reverse vocabulary?",a:"The model outputs an ID; we need to translate that ID back into a readable word."},{q:"What word does ID 6 map to?",a:"the (lowercase)."}], rv:{inp:"{The:0, quick:1, …, dog:8}",out:"{0:The, 1:quick, 2:brown, 3:fox, 4:jumps, 5:over, 6:the, 7:lazy, 8:dog}",q:"What happened here?",a:"The vocabulary was inverted into an ID→word table, so model outputs can be read as words."}, mem:{one:"Flip the dictionary backwards.",img:"A decoder ring for numbers.",eq:"word ↔ ID",oneR:"Generation translates the winning ID back into a word.",recall:"Which ID maps back to the word 'dog'?"}},

{n:6, t:"Embeddings", p:"Replace each word ID with a learned list of 4 numbers.", w:"IDs are arbitrary — ID 7 is not 'more' than ID 2 in any meaningful way. Embeddings give every word a small vector of learned numbers so the network can compute with meaning. The embedding matrix is 9 rows × 4 columns, initialized randomly and improved during training.", inp:"A token ID (0–8).", proc:"Look up the ID's row in the embedding matrix. For the first training example, input The = ID 0, whose embedding row is fixed and verified.", out:"A 4-number vector for the input word.", m:["Embedding lookup","word ID → row of the 9×4 matrix"], flow:[["INPUT · embedding of The","0.278854, −0.949978, −0.449941, −0.553579",""]], eli5:"Every word gets its own secret recipe of 4 numbers. The network stirs recipes instead of words.", pic:"A spice rack with 9 jars; each jar (word) holds 4 measured pinches of flavor (numbers).", rem:["Embedding size = 4 numbers per word.","The embedding of The is [0.278854, −0.949978, −0.449941, −0.553579]."], qz:[{q:"Why not feed raw word IDs into the network?",a:"IDs are arbitrary labels with no meaning. Embeddings give each word learnable numbers the network can compute with."},{q:"What is the embedding of 'The'?",a:"[0.278854, −0.949978, −0.449941, −0.553579]"}], rv:{inp:"word ID 0 (The)",out:"[0.278854, −0.949978, −0.449941, −0.553579]",q:"What happened here?",a:"The word ID was replaced by its embedding — a row of 4 learned numbers from the 9×4 embedding matrix."}, mem:{one:"Words become 4-number recipes.",img:"A spice rack of number jars.",eq:"ID 0 → [0.278854, −0.949978, −0.449941, −0.553579]",oneR:"Embedding size is 4 — tiny on purpose.",recall:"How many numbers make up one embedding?"}},

{n:7, t:"First Neuron", p:"Multiply the embedding by weights, add them up, add a bias — one neuron's pre-activation z.", w:"This is the atomic unit of every neural network: a weighted sum plus bias. The first hidden neuron mixes the 4 embedding numbers into a single score. Every weight decides how much each input number matters; the bias shifts the result up or down.", inp:"Embedding of The: [0.278854, −0.949978, −0.449941, −0.553579] plus 4 weights and 1 bias for neuron 0.", proc:"Multiply each input by its weight, sum the products, then add the bias. Verified numbers for hidden neuron 0:", out:"The pre-activation z of neuron 0: +0.384029.", m:["Weighted sum + bias","z = x₁w₁ + x₂w₂ + x₃w₃ + x₄w₄ + b"], flow:[["WEIGHTED SUM","0.918447",""],["BIAS","−0.534418","+"],["z (PRE-ACTIVATION)","+0.384029","→"]], eli5:"A smoothie maker: pour in 4 ingredients (embedding numbers), turn 4 dials (weights) to decide how much of each, blend, then add a pinch of salt (bias).", pic:"Four streams pouring into one cup through adjustable valves; a marked line shows the final fill level z.", rem:["Neuron = weighted sum + bias.","Neuron 0: sum 0.918447, bias −0.534418, z = +0.384029."], qz:[{q:"What two things does a neuron compute?",a:"A weighted sum of its inputs, plus a bias."},{q:"What is z for hidden neuron 0?",a:"z = 0.918447 + (−0.534418) = +0.384029."}], rv:{inp:"weighted sum 0.918447, bias −0.534418",out:"z = +0.384029",q:"What happened here?",a:"The weighted sum and bias were combined into the neuron's pre-activation z. Next, ReLU decides whether z passes through."}, mem:{one:"Neuron = mix inputs, add bias.",img:"A smoothie maker with 4 valves.",eq:"z = x·w + b",oneR:"Neuron 0's z is +0.384029.",recall:"What are the two ingredients of every neuron's calculation?"}},

{n:8, t:"ReLU", p:"Keep positive z values, squash negative ones to zero.", w:"A weighted sum is just a straight line — stacking more of them would change nothing. ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit) clips negatives to zero, introducing a simple non-linearity that lets the network learn curved, interesting patterns.", inp:"z values of the 4 hidden neurons.", proc:"Apply max(0, z) to each: positive z passes through unchanged, negative z becomes 0.", out:"4 activation values — the hidden layer's output.", m:["ReLU","ReLU(z) = max(0, z)"], flow:[["z","+0.384029","ReLU"],["OUTPUT","+0.384029","unchanged"],["z","−0.032770","ReLU"],["OUTPUT","0","blocked"]], eli5:"A one-way gate: positive numbers may pass, negative numbers are stopped at the door and become zero.", pic:"A turnstile that only rotates forward. Positive visitors walk through; negative ones bounce off and vanish.", rem:["ReLU(z) = max(0, z).","Neuron 1 (z = −0.032770) outputs 0; neuron 3 (z = +0.796667) outputs 0.796667."], qz:[{q:"What does ReLU do to a negative number?",a:"Turns it into zero. Positive numbers pass through unchanged."},{q:"Why does the network need ReLU at all?",a:"Without a non-linearity, stacked layers would collapse into one straight-line calculation."}], rv:{inp:"z = −0.032770",out:"0",q:"What happened here?",a:"The hidden neuron's pre-activation was negative, so ReLU blocked it and output 0."}, mem:{one:"Negatives become zero.",img:"A one-way turnstile for numbers.",eq:"ReLU(z) = max(0, z)",oneR:"ReLU adds the bend that makes learning possible.",recall:"What is ReLU(−0.032770)?"}},

{n:9, t:"Hidden Layer", p:"Run all 4 neurons and collect their ReLU outputs — the model's internal representation.", w:"One neuron is a simple mixer; four neurons working in parallel create a richer, 4-dimensional snapshot of the input word. This hidden vector is the model's 'thinking' — a compact internal representation that the output layer will score.", inp:"Embedding of The (4 numbers).", proc:"Each of the 4 hidden neurons computes its weighted sum + bias, then ReLU. Neurons 1 and 2 output 0; neurons 0 and 3 stay positive.", out:"Hidden output vector [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667]. Also record ReLU derivatives [1, 0, 0, 1] — they will matter during backpropagation.", m:["Hidden layer","h = ReLU(W₁x + b₁)  →  4 numbers"], flow:[["NEURON 0","0.384029",""],["NEURON 1","0",""],["NEURON 2","0",""],["NEURON 3","0.796667",""],["HIDDEN OUTPUT","[0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667]","→"]], eli5:"Four judges each taste the word and hold up a score card. Two judges show zero — they had nothing to say about this word.", pic:"Four gauge dials on a machine panel; two needles pinned at zero, two pointing at 0.38 and 0.80.", rem:["Hidden size = 4 neurons.","Hidden output: [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667]. ReLU derivatives: [1, 0, 0, 1]."], qz:[{q:"What is the hidden output for input 'The'?",a:"[0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667] — neurons 1 and 2 were zeroed by ReLU."},{q:"Why do we record ReLU derivatives [1,0,0,1]?",a:"They remember which neurons were open (1) or blocked (0), which is needed later in backpropagation."}], rv:{inp:"z = [0.384029, −0.032770, −0.057852, 0.796667]",out:"[0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667]",q:"What happened here?",a:"ReLU was applied to all four pre-activations: the two negative values became zero, the two positive values passed through."}, mem:{one:"Four neurons → one 4-number thought.",img:"Four score cards held up by judges.",eq:"h = [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667]",oneR:"Hidden output = ReLU applied to each neuron.",recall:"Which hidden neurons output zero for input 'The'?"}},

{n:10, t:"Output Layer", p:"Score every vocabulary word: multiply the hidden vector by the output weights and add biases — 9 raw logits.", w:"The hidden layer understood the input; now the model must rank all 9 possible next words. The output layer is a second weighted-sum stage: 9 neurons, one per vocabulary word, each producing a raw score called a logit.", inp:"Hidden output [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667], plus output weights (4×9 matrix) and 9 output biases.", proc:"For each output neuron j: logitⱼ = h·wⱼ + bⱼ — a weighted sum over the 4 hidden values plus a bias. All 9 logits are computed at once.", out:"9 raw logits — one score per vocabulary word. Logits can be any real number, positive or negative.", m:["Output layer","logitⱼ = h₁w₁ⱼ + h₂w₂ⱼ + h₃w₃ⱼ + h₄w₄ⱼ + bⱼ"], eli5:"Nine judges each weigh the evidence (hidden vector) and hold up a score card. Scores are not percentages yet — just raw points.", pic:"Nine scoreboards in a row, each blinking a raw number, waiting to be converted into chances.", rem:["Output size = 9 logits, one per word.","Logits are un-normalized scores; softmax comes next."], qz:[{q:"How many logits does the output layer produce?",a:"9 — one raw score per vocabulary word."},{q:"Are logits probabilities?",a:"No. They are raw scores; only softmax turns them into probabilities."}], rv:{inp:"h = [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667]",out:"9 raw logits (one per word)",q:"What happened here?",a:"The hidden vector was passed through the output layer, producing 9 raw logits — one score per vocabulary word."}, mem:{one:"Nine judges score the hidden thought.",img:"Nine scoreboards blinking raw numbers.",eq:"logit = h·W₂ + b₂",oneR:"Output layer turns a 4-number thought into 9 word scores.",recall:"What do we call the raw scores from the output layer?"}},

{n:11, t:"Softmax", p:"Turn the 9 logits into probabilities that sum to 1.", w:"Raw logits cannot be compared against a target as 'chances'. Softmax exponentiates each logit (making everything positive), then divides by the total, so the results become true probabilities. At initialization, the model has no clue: it gives the correct next word, quick, only P = 0.073655.", inp:"9 logits.", proc:"Exponentiate each logit, sum them, divide each by the sum. The largest logit gets the largest probability; all 9 probabilities add to exactly 1.", out:"A probability distribution over the 9 words. Initially P(quick) = 0.073655 — a poor guess.", m:["Softmax","P(tokenᵢ) = exp(logitᵢ) / Σ exp(logit)"], flow:[["LOGIT of quick","low raw score","exp"],["SUM of exp over 9 words","S",""],["P(quick)","0.073655","= ÷"]], eli5:"Turning judges' score cards into raffle tickets: the best score gets the most tickets, and all tickets together fill one jar.", pic:"Nine faucets pouring into one tank; each faucet's flow width matches its probability.", rem:["Probabilities are positive and sum to 1.","Initially P(quick) = 0.073655 — barely better than random (1/9 ≈ 0.111 would be uniform; the model starts near-random)."], qz:[{q:"What does softmax guarantee about its outputs?",a:"They are all positive and sum to 1 — a valid probability distribution."},{q:"What was P(quick) at the start?",a:"0.073655 — a weak initial guess."}], rv:{inp:"9 raw logits",out:"9 probabilities summing to 1",q:"What happened here?",a:"Softmax converted the logits into a probability distribution — the model's prediction in percentage form."}, mem:{one:"Scores become chances.",img:"Score cards turned into raffle tickets.",eq:"Pᵢ = exp(zᵢ) / Σ exp(z)",oneR:"Initial P(quick) = 0.073655 — the model barely knows anything yet.",recall:"What is the sum of all softmax probabilities?"}},

{n:12, t:"Loss", p:"Measure exactly how wrong the prediction was — one number.", w:"Learning needs a target signal: a single number that says 'this prediction was bad, and here is how bad.' Cross-entropy loss looks only at the probability the model gave the correct word and takes −log of it. Give the right answer a tiny probability and the loss explodes; give it probability 1 and the loss is 0.", inp:"The prediction (probabilities) and the correct target word quick (P = 0.073655).", proc:"Loss = −log(P(target)). With P(quick) = 0.073655: Loss = −log(0.073655) = 2.608369.", out:"Loss = 2.608369. High number = big mistake.", m:["Cross entropy","Loss = −log(P(target))"], flow:[["P(correct word quick)","0.073655","−log"],["LOSS","2.608369","→"]], eli5:"A penalty meter: the smaller the chance you gave the right answer, the harder the machine slaps the meter.", pic:"A speedometer labeled 'wrongness' with the needle pinned near the red zone at 2.61.", rem:["Loss = −log(P(correct)).","Initial loss: 2.608369 with P(quick) = 0.073655."], qz:[{q:"What does the loss measure?",a:"How wrong the prediction was, based only on the probability given to the correct word."},{q:"What is the initial loss?",a:"2.608369, from −log(0.073655)."}], rv:{inp:"P(quick) = 0.073655, target = quick",out:"Loss = 2.608369",q:"What happened here?",a:"Cross-entropy converted the prediction into a single wrongness number: −log of the probability given to the correct word."}, mem:{one:"One number says how wrong we are.",img:"A wrongness speedometer near the red zone.",eq:"Loss = −log(P(target)) = 2.608369",oneR:"High loss = big mistake; the goal is to shrink it.",recall:"What was the loss at the start of training?"}},

{n:13, t:"Logit Gradients", p:"Compute how each logit should change to reduce the loss.", w:"Before blaming any weight, we ask a simpler question: if we could nudge each output score directly, which way would reduce the loss? For softmax + cross-entropy the answer is beautifully simple: gradient = probability − target. One-hot target means the correct word gets p − 1 (a strong push up) and every other word gets its own p (a push down).", inp:"Softmax probabilities and the one-hot target vector.", proc:"For the target word quick: gradient = 0.073655 − 1 = −0.926345 (must rise). Every other word w: gradient = P(w) (must fall).", out:"9 logit gradients — a direction for every score.", m:["Logit gradient","gradient = probability − target"], flow:[["P(quick)","0.073655",""],["TARGET","1","−"],["LOGIT GRADIENT","−0.926345","→"]], eli5:"The scorekeeper writes a correction note on every judge's card: 'you were too low' or 'you were too high', with exact amounts.", pic:"Nine judges handed sticky notes: the correct word's judge gets a big upward arrow, the rest get small downward arrows.", rem:["gradient = probability − target.","quick's gradient: −0.926345 — it needs to rise a lot."], qz:[{q:"What is the logit gradient formula?",a:"gradient = probability − target."},{q:"What is the gradient for the correct word quick?",a:"0.073655 − 1 = −0.926345 — a strong signal that its score must increase."}], rv:{inp:"P(quick) = 0.073655, target = 1",out:"logit gradient = −0.926345",q:"What happened here?",a:"The softmax+cross-entropy gradient was computed: probability minus target. The negative value says the correct word's score must rise."}, mem:{one:"Gradient = guess − truth, per logit.",img:"Sticky notes correcting nine judges.",eq:"∂L/∂logit = p − y",oneR:"quick's gradient −0.926345 means 'push this score up.'",recall:"What is the gradient of the correct word's logit?"}},

{n:14, t:"Output Weight Gradients", p:"Translate logit blame into weight blame: gradient = logit gradient × hidden value.", w:"We cannot edit logits directly — they come from weights. The chain rule says: a weight's responsibility = (how much its logit was off) × (how much that weight contributed, i.e., its input). If a hidden value is 0, the weight above it gets zero blame.", inp:"Logit gradients (from Step 13) and hidden output [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667].", proc:"For output weight connecting hidden neuron i to output word j: weight gradient = logit-gradientⱼ × hᵢ. Hidden neurons 1 and 2 output 0, so all weights feeding from them get gradient 0 this pass.", out:"A 4×9 matrix of output weight gradients — blame assigned to every output weight.", m:["Output weight gradient","∂L/∂w = logit gradient × hidden value"], flow:[["LOGIT GRADIENT","−0.926345",""],["HIDDEN VALUE h₀","0.384029","×"],["WEIGHT GRADIENT","−0.355709","→"]], eli5:"If a judge was wrong, blame = how wrong they were × how loudly each witness spoke. A silent witness (zero) receives no blame.", pic:"Blame flowing backward along wires; wires from silent neurons carry nothing.", rem:["weight gradient = logit gradient × hidden value.","Zero hidden values mean zero gradients for weights above them."], qz:[{q:"How is an output weight's gradient computed?",a:"logit gradient × hidden value."},{q:"Which output weights get zero gradient this pass?",a:"All weights coming from hidden neurons 1 and 2, because their outputs are 0."}], rv:{inp:"logit grad −0.926345, hidden value 0.384029",out:"weight gradient −0.355709",q:"What happened here?",a:"The chain rule multiplied the logit gradient by the hidden value, converting logit blame into weight blame (−0.926345 × 0.384029 = −0.355709)."}, mem:{one:"Blame flows: logit grad × input.",img:"Blame traveling backward along wires.",eq:"∂L/∂w = δ × h",oneR:"Zero input means zero gradient for that weight.",recall:"What two quantities multiply to give a weight gradient?"}},

{n:15, t:"Output Bias Gradients", p:"Each output bias takes the full blame of its logit.", w:"A bias is added directly to its logit — no multiplication by an input — so the chain rule gives it the logit's entire gradient. The correct word's bias receives −0.926345, the strongest correction of all.", inp:"The 9 logit gradients.", proc:"bias gradientⱼ = logit gradientⱼ (the input to a bias is effectively 1).", out:"9 output bias gradients — one per vocabulary word.", m:["Output bias gradient","bias gradient = logit gradient"], flow:[["LOGIT GRADIENT of quick","−0.926345","="],["BIAS GRADIENT","−0.926345","→"]], eli5:"A bias is a judge's personal head start — so when the judge is wrong, the head start takes the whole blame.", pic:"A judge's adjustable chair height taking the full correction note.", rem:["bias gradient = logit gradient.","quick's bias gradient: −0.926345."], qz:[{q:"Why does a bias get the full logit gradient?",a:"A bias contributes directly (multiplied by 1), so the chain rule leaves the gradient unchanged."},{q:"What is the bias gradient for quick?",a:"−0.926345."}], rv:{inp:"logit gradient −0.926345",out:"bias gradient −0.926345",q:"What happened here?",a:"The bias inherited the logit's full gradient — biases contribute directly, so their gradient equals the logit gradient."}, mem:{one:"Bias takes the logit's full blame.",img:"A judge's chair height corrected fully.",eq:"∂L/∂b = δ",oneR:"bias gradient = logit gradient, always.",recall:"What is the relationship between a bias gradient and its logit gradient?"}},

{n:16, t:"Learning Rate", p:"Choose how big each corrective step will be: 0.1.", w:"Gradients say which direction to move, not how far. The learning rate converts gradient into an actual step. Our model uses 0.1 — each parameter moves one tenth of its gradient per update. Too large a rate overshoots and destabilizes; too small makes training crawl.", inp:"Gradients (blame) for every parameter.", proc:"Multiply every gradient by the learning rate 0.1 to get the actual change size, then subtract from the parameter.", out:"A fixed, safe step size used by every update in this course.", m:["Learning rate","step = learning rate × gradient = 0.1 × gradient"], flow:[["GRADIENT","−0.355709",""],["LEARNING RATE","0.1","×"],["STEP SIZE","−0.035571","→"]], eli5:"Walking down a hill in fog: the gradient points downhill, and the learning rate decides whether you take baby steps or leap.", pic:"A hiker on a foggy slope taking small, careful steps downhill.", rem:["Learning rate = 0.1 in this model.","Gradient = direction; learning rate = distance."], qz:[{q:"What does the learning rate control?",a:"How far each parameter moves against its gradient — the step size."},{q:"What learning rate does our model use?",a:"0.1"}], rv:{inp:"gradient −0.355709",out:"step −0.035571",q:"What happened here?",a:"The gradient was scaled by the learning rate 0.1, producing the actual parameter change (−0.355709 × 0.1 = −0.035571)."}, mem:{one:"Gradient × 0.1 = the step.",img:"Careful steps down a foggy hill.",eq:"Δ = η · g,  η = 0.1",oneR:"Learning rate = 0.1 everywhere in this course.",recall:"What is this model's learning rate?"}},

{n:17, t:"First Parameter Update", p:"Change one weight for real: new = old − learning rate × gradient.", w:"This is the moment the model actually learns — its first ever modification of internal numbers. We subtract (learning rate × gradient) from the parameter, moving it against the gradient, downhill on the loss. For a negative gradient, subtracting means the weight grows.", inp:"Old weight, its gradient (−0.355709), learning rate 0.1.", proc:"new = old − 0.1 × gradient = old − 0.1 × (−0.355709) = old + 0.035571.", out:"One updated weight, slightly larger than before.", m:["Parameter update","new = old − learning rate × gradient"], flow:[["OLD WEIGHT","w (any value)",""],["LEARNING RATE × GRADIENT","−0.035571","−"],["NEW WEIGHT","w + 0.035571","→"]], eli5:"Turning a dial a tiny notch in the direction that shrinks the mistake.", pic:"A hand gripping a dial marked 'w' and rotating it a quarter-notch.", rem:["Update direction is OPPOSITE the gradient.","new = old − 0.1 × gradient."], qz:[{q:"Why do we SUBTRACT the gradient?",a:"The gradient points uphill on loss; subtracting moves downhill, reducing the mistake."},{q:"A weight has gradient −0.355709. How does it change?",a:"new = old − 0.1×(−0.355709) = old + 0.035571 — it increases."}], rv:{inp:"gradient −0.355709, learning rate 0.1",out:"weight increases by 0.035571",q:"What happened here?",a:"The parameter update ran: old − learning rate × gradient. A negative gradient made the weight grow, pushing its logit up toward the target."}, mem:{one:"Nudge every dial against its blame.",img:"A hand turning a dial a quarter-notch.",eq:"w ← w − η·g",oneR:"Subtract the gradient — always move downhill.",recall:"Which way do we move relative to the gradient?"}},

{n:18, t:"Verify Learning Update", p:"Re-run the forward pass and confirm the update actually helped.", w:"Never trust math you have not checked. After updating the output layer's weights and biases, we repeat the forward pass for the same example (The → quick) and re-measure P(quick). The probability rises sharply — the first concrete proof that the update worked.", inp:"Updated parameters; same example: The → quick.", proc:"Forward pass again: embedding → hidden → logits → softmax. Compare new P(quick) with the old 0.073655.", out:"P(quick) is much higher than before — learning verified for one example.", m:["Verification","P_new(quick) ≫ P_old(quick) = 0.073655"], eli5:"Practice one piano scale, then play it again to hear that it improved.", pic:"A before/after gauge where the needle swings from 0.07 toward 0.5.", rem:["Always re-run the forward pass after updating.","The update helped: P(quick) increased."], qz:[{q:"How do we verify an update worked?",a:"Re-run the forward pass on the same example and check that the target's probability increased."},{q:"What was P(quick) before this update?",a:"0.073655."}], rv:{inp:"P(quick) before = 0.073655",out:"P(quick) after update: noticeably higher",q:"What happened here?",a:"The forward pass was repeated after the parameter update, and the target probability rose — evidence the update reduced the loss."}, mem:{one:"Update, then re-check the prediction.",img:"A gauge needle swinging right.",eq:"check: P_new(quick) > 0.073655",oneR:"Verification proves the update reduced the mistake.",recall:"How do we confirm a parameter update helped?"}},

{n:19, t:"Training Loop", p:"Repeat forever: forward pass → loss → backward pass → update.", w:"One update fixes one example slightly. Learning happens by looping: predict, measure the mistake, assign blame backwards, adjust, and try again. Each cycle is cheap; thousands of cycles compound into real skill.", inp:"All 8 training examples (eventually), the full model.", proc:"Cycle: (1) forward pass computes prediction; (2) loss measures error; (3) backward pass computes gradients; (4) update moves parameters. Repeat.", out:"A trained model — parameters that make the loss smaller over time.", m:["Training loop","predict → measure → blame → adjust → repeat"], eli5:"Shooting baskets: shoot, miss, adjust your arm, shoot again — ten thousand times.", pic:"A circular conveyor belt carrying a ball through four stations: guess, score, diagnose, fix.", rem:["Training = repetition of the full loop.","Forward pass answers; backward pass blames; update fixes."], qz:[{q:"What are the four stages of each training iteration?",a:"Forward pass (predict), loss (measure), backward pass (blame via gradients), parameter update (adjust)."},{q:"Why loop instead of updating once?",a:"One update barely moves the loss; many small updates compound into real learning."}], rv:{inp:"model + 8 examples",out:"gradually decreasing loss",q:"What happened here?",a:"The four-stage cycle (forward, loss, backward, update) was repeated across examples — that repetition is training."}, mem:{one:"Predict, measure, blame, adjust — repeat.",img:"A conveyor belt of four stations.",eq:"loop(forward, loss, backward, update)",oneR:"Learning is repetition of the full loop.",recall:"Name the four stages of one training iteration."}},

{n:20, t:"Verify Hidden Forward Pass", p:"Hand-check all 4 hidden neurons: weighted sum, bias, z, and ReLU output for input The.", w:"Before trusting backpropagation through the hidden layer, we verify the hidden forward pass with exact numbers. These verified values anchor everything: hidden output [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667] and ReLU derivatives [1, 0, 0, 1].", inp:"Embedding of The: [0.278854, −0.949978, −0.449941, −0.553579].", proc:"For each hidden neuron: weighted sum → add bias → z → ReLU. Neuron 1's z is −0.032770, so ReLU outputs 0; neuron 3's z is +0.796667, so it passes through.", out:"Verified hidden output [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667] with ReLU derivatives [1, 0, 0, 1].", m:["Hidden verification","h = ReLU(W₁x + b₁)"], flow:[["NEURON 0 · sum","0.918447","+ bias −0.534418"],["NEURON 0 · z","+0.384029","ReLU → 0.384029"],["NEURON 1 · z","−0.032770","ReLU → 0"],["NEURON 2 · z","−0.057852","ReLU → 0"],["NEURON 3 · z","+0.796667","ReLU → 0.796667"],["HIDDEN OUTPUT","[0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667]","derivatives [1,0,0,1]"]], eli5:"Checking four thermometers by hand before you trust the automatic weather report.", pic:"Four dials on a panel, each annotated with sum, bias, z, and final output in fine pencil.", rem:["Neuron 0: sum 0.918447, bias −0.534418, z +0.384029, out 0.384029.","Neurons 1 and 2 output 0; neuron 3 outputs 0.796667.","ReLU derivatives [1, 0, 0, 1] = which gates were open."], qz:[{q:"What is z and the ReLU output for hidden neuron 3?",a:"z = +0.796667; ReLU keeps it, so output = 0.796667."},{q:"What are the ReLU derivatives and what do they mean?",a:"[1, 0, 0, 1] — neurons 0 and 3 were open (passed values), neurons 1 and 2 were blocked."}], rv:{inp:"z = +0.384029 (neuron 0)",out:"0.384029",q:"What happened here?",a:"The hidden neuron's pre-activation was positive, so ReLU allowed the value through unchanged."}, mem:{one:"Four neurons hand-verified for 'The'.",img:"Four annotated dials in fine pencil.",eq:"h = [0.384029, 0, 0, 0.796667]",oneR:"These exact numbers anchor all hidden-layer backprop.",recall:"What is the verified hidden output for input 'The'?"}},

{n:21, t:"Hidden-Layer Backpropagation", p:"Propagate the blame backward through the output layer into the hidden neurons.", w:"Gradients so far only blamed the output layer. To fix the hidden layer — and eventually the embeddings — we must send the blame backward: hidden gradient = (sum of logit gradients × output weights reaching this neuron) × ReLU derivative. This is backpropagation through the hidden layer.", inp:"Logit gradients, output weights W₂, and ReLU derivatives [1, 0, 0, 1].", proc:"For hidden neuron i: backprop sum = Σⱼ (logit-gradientⱼ × W₂[i][j]); then multiply by ReLU derivative (0 for blocked neurons — no blame can pass a closed gate).", out:"4 hidden-neuron gradients — the hidden layer's share of the blame.", m:["Hidden gradient","δ_h = (δ_logits · W₂ᵀ) ⊙ ReLU′(z)"], eli5:"Blame flows backward through the wires that carried signals forward — but only through open gates; closed gates (ReLU zeros) stop blame dead.", pic:"Red arrows traveling in reverse along the network's wires, dissolving at the shut gates of neurons 1 and 2.", rem:["Blame flows backward along the same weights.","Closed ReLU gates (derivative 0) block all backward blame."], qz:[{q:"Why do neurons 1 and 2 receive zero hidden gradient?",a:"Their ReLU derivatives are 0 — a closed gate passes no signal forward and no blame backward."},{q:"What two factors multiply to give a hidden gradient?",a:"The back-propagated sum (logit gradients × output weights) and the ReLU derivative."}], rv:{inp:"logit grads × W₂, ReLU derivative 0",out:"hidden gradient 0",q:"What happened here?",a:"Backprop reached a blocked neuron: its ReLU derivative was 0, so it received zero gradient — no blame passes a closed gate."}, mem:{one:"Send blame backward through open gates only.",img:"Red arrows dissolving at shut gates.",eq:"δ_h = (δ · W₂ᵀ) ⊙ ReLU′",oneR:"ReLU′ = 0 means zero blame for that neuron.",recall:"What happens to blame at a neuron whose ReLU output was 0?"}},

{n:22, t:"Hidden-Weight Gradients", p:"Assign blame to each hidden-layer weight: hidden gradient × input embedding value.", w:"Now that each hidden neuron carries its gradient, we can blame its weights the same way we blamed output weights: weight gradient = hidden gradient × input value. This reaches all the way back to the embedding of The.", inp:"4 hidden gradients and the embedding [0.278854, −0.949978, −0.449941, −0.553579].", proc:"For hidden weight connecting input i to hidden neuron j: gradient = hidden-gradientⱼ × xᵢ.", out:"A 4×4 matrix of hidden weight gradients — blame for the first layer of the network.", m:["Hidden weight gradient","∂L/∂W₁ = hidden gradient ⊗ input"], flow:[["HIDDEN GRADIENT","δ (per neuron)",""],["INPUT VALUE","0.278854","×"],["HIDDEN WEIGHT GRADIENT","δ × 0.278854","→"]], eli5:"Same rule as before, one layer deeper: blame = how wrong × how much input arrived.", pic:"Blame arrows continuing backward from hidden neurons to the four embedding dials.", rem:["Same chain-rule pattern at every layer: gradient × input.","Hidden-weight gradients let the model fix how words are mixed."], qz:[{q:"How is a hidden weight's gradient computed?",a:"hidden gradient × the input value feeding that weight."},{q:"What is the chain-rule pattern that repeats at every layer?",a:"weight gradient = (gradient of what is above) × (input from below)."}], rv:{inp:"hidden gradient δ, input 0.278854",out:"weight gradient = δ × 0.278854",q:"What happened here?",a:"The chain rule assigned blame to a hidden weight by multiplying the hidden neuron's gradient by its input value — the same pattern used for output weights."}, mem:{one:"Same rule, deeper layer: δ × input.",img:"Blame arrows reaching the embedding dials.",eq:"∂L/∂W₁ = δ_h ⊗ x",oneR:"One chain-rule pattern handles every weight in the network.",recall:"What two values multiply to give a hidden-weight gradient?"}},

{n:23, t:"Hidden-Bias Gradients", p:"Each hidden bias takes its neuron's full gradient.", w:"Hidden biases behave exactly like output biases: they are added directly, so each inherits its neuron's complete gradient. Two of the four will be zero — their neurons' ReLU gates were closed.", inp:"The 4 hidden gradients.", proc:"hidden-bias-gradientᵢ = hidden-gradientᵢ (input is effectively 1).", out:"4 hidden bias gradients — two of them zero (neurons 1 and 2).", m:["Hidden bias gradient","∂L/∂b₁ = hidden gradient"], flow:[["HIDDEN GRADIENT (neuron 1)","δ (blocked gate → 0)",""],["BIAS GRADIENT","0","→"]], eli5:"Same as the output biases: the head start takes the whole correction note — but a silenced neuron has no note at all.", pic:"Four chair-height knobs; two get adjustment notes, two sit untouched.", rem:["bias gradient = hidden gradient.","Neurons 1 and 2: bias gradients 0 (closed gates)."], qz:[{q:"What are the hidden bias gradients for neurons 1 and 2?",a:"0 — their ReLU gates were closed, so their gradients are zero."},{q:"Why does a bias gradient equal its neuron's gradient?",a:"The bias contributes directly (×1), so the chain rule changes nothing."}], rv:{inp:"hidden gradient 0 (blocked neuron)",out:"bias gradient 0",q:"What happened here?",a:"The hidden bias inherited its neuron's gradient — and since the ReLU gate was closed, both are zero."}, mem:{one:"Bias takes its neuron's full gradient.",img:"Two knobs adjusted, two untouched.",eq:"∂L/∂b₁ = δ_h",oneR:"Blocked neurons get zero bias gradient.",recall:"Why are two hidden bias gradients zero?"}},

{n:24, t:"Hidden-Layer Parameter Update", p:"Apply the update rule to hidden weights and biases: w ← w − 0.1 × gradient.", w:"Blame is only useful if we act on it. With hidden gradients in hand, we finally update the first layer of the network — the same subtraction rule as Step 17, now reaching the weights that mix the embedding itself.", inp:"Hidden weight gradients (4×4) and hidden bias gradients (4), learning rate 0.1.", proc:"For every hidden weight and bias: new = old − 0.1 × gradient. Blocked neurons' parameters stay unchanged this pass.", out:"Updated hidden layer — the network now mixes inputs differently.", m:["Hidden update","W₁ ← W₁ − 0.1·∂L/∂W₁   ·   b₁ ← b₁ − 0.1·∂L/∂b₁"], eli5:"Finally turning the inner dials, not just the outer ones.", pic:"A technician reaching inside the machine to adjust the first bank of dials.", rem:["Same update rule at every layer.","Parameters above closed ReLU gates do not move this pass."], qz:[{q:"What is the update rule for hidden weights?",a:"new = old − 0.1 × gradient — identical to the output layer update."},{q:"Which hidden parameters stay unchanged this pass?",a:"Those belonging to neurons 1 and 2, whose gradients are zero."}], rv:{inp:"hidden gradient δ, learning rate 0.1",out:"W₁, b₁ updated (zeros stay fixed)",q:"What happened here?",a:"The parameter update was applied to the hidden layer: every weight and bias moved by −0.1 × its gradient; zero-gradient parameters stayed put."}, mem:{one:"Turn the inner dials too.",img:"A hand reaching inside the machine.",eq:"W₁ ← W₁ − 0.1·g",oneR:"One update rule serves every parameter.",recall:"Which hidden parameters do not change this pass, and why?"}},

{n:25, t:"Update Everything Together", p:"Update all four parameter groups — W₁, b₁, W₂, b₂ — in a single training step.", w:"A real training step touches everything at once: after one forward and one backward pass, all 4×4 hidden weights, 4 hidden biases, 4×9 output weights, and 9 output biases move together. (We keep the embedding matrix frozen in this course to keep the arithmetic small.)", inp:"Gradients for every parameter group.", proc:"Apply w ← w − 0.1 × g to W₁, b₁, W₂, b₂ simultaneously, using gradients from the same backward pass.", out:"One fully updated model, ready for the next example.", m:["Full update","every parameter ← itself − 0.1 × its gradient"], eli5:"Tuning the whole engine in one go, not one screw at a time.", pic:"Four banks of dials rotating together in one smooth motion.", rem:["One step = one simultaneous update of all parameter groups.","Embeddings stay frozen in this course for simplicity."], qz:[{q:"Which parameter groups update together?",a:"W₁, b₁, W₂, b₂ — all in the same step."},{q:"Why are embeddings kept frozen here?",a:"To keep the arithmetic small and inspectable; real models also train embeddings."}], rv:{inp:"gradients for W₁, b₁, W₂, b₂",out:"all four groups updated",q:"What happened here?",a:"A complete training step: every parameter group was updated simultaneously from one backward pass."}, mem:{one:"All dials turn at once.",img:"Four dial banks rotating together.",eq:"θ ← θ − η·∇L(θ)",oneR:"One training step updates every parameter group together.",recall:"Which four parameter groups update in one step?"}},

{n:26, t:"Train Across All 8 Examples", p:"Run the full loop once per training example — that is one epoch.", w:"The model must not just memorize one pair; it needs to do well on all 8 (The→quick, quick→brown, …, lazy→dog). One epoch = forward, loss, backward, and update for each example in turn. Each example pulls the parameters a little in its own direction.", inp:"All 8 training examples and the current model.", proc:"Loop over the 8 examples: for each, run forward pass, compute loss, backpropagate, update. Repeat for many epochs.", out:"A model balanced across the entire sentence — better on every word, not just one.", m:["Epoch","1 epoch = 8 examples × (forward + loss + backward + update)"], eli5:"Practicing every flashcard in the deck once, instead of drilling the same card all day.", pic:"A card carousel feeding eight flashcards through the four-station loop, one after another.", rem:["Epoch = one pass through all 8 examples.","Each example nudges parameters toward its own target."], qz:[{q:"What is an epoch?",a:"One complete pass through all 8 training examples."},{q:"Why train on all examples instead of just one?",a:"Each example pulls parameters its own way; training on all of them balances the model across the whole sentence."}], rv:{inp:"8 examples",out:"8 updates per epoch",q:"What happened here?",a:"The training loop ran across every example — 8 forward/backward/update cycles made up one epoch."}, mem:{one:"One loop per flashcard, every card.",img:"A carousel of eight flashcards.",eq:"1 epoch = 8 × (fwd + bwd + update)",oneR:"An epoch visits every example once.",recall:"How many training iterations make one epoch here?"}},

{n:27, t:"Check Whether the Model Learned", p:"Compare the verified checkpoints: did loss fall and did P(quick) rise?", w:"Numbers, not vibes. At step 1 the loss was 2.608369 with P(quick) = 0.073655. By step 20 the verified loss is 0.549986 and P(quick) reaches 0.576958 — the model now picks quick over half the time. Honest caveat: this is a 9-word toy; it demonstrates the mechanics of learning, not useful language ability.", inp:"Verified metrics from early and later training.", proc:"Compare checkpoints (each recorded just BEFORE its update): loss 2.608369 → 0.549986; P(quick) 0.073655 → 0.576958.", out:"Loss −2.058383; P(quick) +0.503304. Learning confirmed — mechanically.", m:["Learning check","Δloss = −2.058383   ·   ΔP(quick) = +0.503304"], flow:[["LOSS at step 1","2.608369",""],["LOSS at step 20","0.549986","−2.058383"],["P(quick) at step 1","0.073655",""],["P(quick) at step 20","0.576958","+0.503304"]], eli5:"Weighing yourself before and after a diet — same scale, same time, to see real change.", pic:"Two photos side by side: a frowning gauge at 2.61 and a calmer one at 0.55.", rem:["Loss fell from 2.608369 to 0.549986.","P(quick) rose from 0.073655 to 0.576958.","Toy model = learning mechanics, not intelligence."], qz:[{q:"By how much did the loss decrease?",a:"2.058383 — from 2.608369 down to 0.549986."},{q:"Why is P(quick) = 0.576958 not 'understanding'?",a:"This 9-word model only demonstrates training mechanics; real language needs vastly more data and scale."}], rv:{inp:"loss 2.608369 → 0.549986",out:"learning confirmed",q:"What happened here?",a:"Loss fell by 2.058383 while P(quick) rose by 0.503304 — verified evidence that the training loop works."}, mem:{one:"Loss down 2.06, P(quick) up 0.50.",img:"A before/after gauge photo.",eq:"2.608 → 0.550 loss; 0.074 → 0.577 P(quick)",oneR:"Metrics prove the mechanics; scale is what real LLMs add.",recall:"What were the loss values at steps 1 and 20?"}},

{n:28, t:"Build Prediction Function", p:"Package the trained forward pass as one function: word in, next-word probabilities out.", w:"Training is over; now we use the machine. The prediction function wraps embedding → hidden → ReLU → output → softmax into a single callable routine — the same forward pass, now treated as a finished product.", inp:"Any single word from the vocabulary.", proc:"Look up its embedding, run the verified forward pass, return the 9 probabilities (or just the argmax — the single most likely next word).", out:"A next-word prediction: e.g. predict(The) → quick with the highest probability.", m:["Prediction","predict(w) = argmax(softmax(W₂·ReLU(W₁·E[w] + b₁) + b₂))"], eli5:"Wrapping the whole machine in a vending machine button: press a word, get the next word.", pic:"A vending machine with word buttons; pressing 'The' drops a card reading 'quick 0.577'.", rem:["Prediction = the trained forward pass, reused.","argmax picks the single most likely next word."], qz:[{q:"What does the prediction function contain?",a:"Exactly the trained forward pass: embedding lookup, hidden layer, ReLU, output layer, softmax."},{q:"What does argmax return?",a:"The single word with the highest probability."}], rv:{inp:"predict(The)",out:"quick (highest probability)",q:"What happened here?",a:"The trained forward pass was packaged as a function: input word The produced quick as the most likely next word."}, mem:{one:"Wrap the forward pass in one button.",img:"A vending machine for next words.",eq:"predict(w) = argmax softmax(...)",oneR:"Training builds the machine; prediction just runs it.",recall:"What is the most likely next word after 'The' in the trained model?"}},

{n:29, t:"Generate Text", p:"Feed each prediction back in as the next input and let the sentence extend itself.", w:"One prediction is nice; a loop of predictions is generation. Take the predicted word, feed it back as the new input, and repeat. The model walks word by word through its own output — this is the seed of how every LLM writes text.", inp:"A starting word (e.g. The) and the prediction function.", proc:"Repeat: predict next word from current word → append it → use it as the new input. Optionally sample probabilistically instead of always taking the argmax.", out:"A generated chain, e.g. The → quick → brown → fox → jumps → over → the → lazy → dog — the model reproducing its training sentence.", m:["Generation","wₜ₊₁ = predict(wₜ), repeated"], eli5:"A game of telephone with yourself: say a word, hear the next, say that one, and keep going.", pic:"A snake of word-cards where each new card is copied from the machine's last output and fed back into its slot.", rem:["Generation = prediction in a feedback loop.","On this tiny model it reproduces its one sentence; sampling adds variety."], qz:[{q:"How does text generation work?",a:"Repeatedly predict the next word and feed that prediction back in as the new input."},{q:"What is the difference between argmax and sampling?",a:"argmax always picks the single best word; sampling rolls weighted dice, adding variety."}], rv:{inp:"start: The",out:"The → quick → brown → fox → …",q:"What happened here?",a:"Predictions were fed back as inputs in a loop — the model generated a chain of words, step by step."}, mem:{one:"Predict, feed back, repeat.",img:"A snake of cards eating its own tail.",eq:"wₜ₊₁ = predict(wₜ)",oneR:"Every LLM writes text by looping prediction.",recall:"What makes generation different from a single prediction?"}},

{n:30, t:"Final End-to-End Test", p:"Run the entire pipeline in one breath: from raw text to generated words.", w:"The last step connects everything: the same sentence becomes tokens, IDs, embeddings, hidden thoughts, logits, probabilities, loss, gradients, updated parameters — and finally new text. If you can narrate this chain, you understand the basic training loop of a language model.", inp:"The training sentence and the fully trained tiny model.", proc:"TEXT → TOKENS → NUMBERS → HIDDEN REPRESENTATION → LOGITS → PROBABILITIES → LOSS → GRADIENTS → UPDATED PARAMETERS → TRAINING → PREDICTION → GENERATED TEXT.", out:"Generated text — plus a complete mental model of a language machine.", m:["The whole machine","text ⇄ numbers, forward to predict, backward to learn"], eli5:"Taking the training wheels off and riding the whole bike in one go.", pic:"The entire machine in one wide diagram, every stage lit up, humming quietly.", rem:["You now understand the basic training loop of a tiny language model.","Forward pass answers · backward pass blames · update fixes · loop repeats."], qz:[{q:"Name the full chain from text to generated text.",a:"Text → tokens → IDs/embeddings → hidden layer (ReLU) → logits → softmax probabilities → loss → gradients → parameter updates → training loop → prediction → generation."},{q:"What are the three distinct passes in training?",a:"Forward pass (calculate), backward pass (assign blame), parameter update (change numbers)."}], rv:{inp:"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog",out:"the model's generated continuation",q:"What happened here?",a:"The complete pipeline ran end to end: text became numbers, numbers became predictions, mistakes became gradients, gradients became updates — and updates became new text."}, mem:{one:"You have seen the whole machine.",img:"Every stage lit up at once.",eq:"text → numbers → prediction → loss → gradients → update → text",oneR:"Forward predicts; backward blames; updates fix; loops repeat.",recall:"What are the three passes, in order?"}}
